hed us as a  professional and that we have to make we  have to be more wary of the firewalls  that should exist back in 1992 I had  covered the Clinton campaign from the  very first day and some of the more  extraordinary experiences were that sort  of rollercoaster of that particular  campaign but in 1992 Bill Clinton was  elected I was in Little Rock covering  the transition and I got a call from my  bureau chief Tim Russert and he said  I've got good news and bad I had been  covering the hill before I covered  Clinton I had been covering the bush the  first Bush transition then covered  Congress for four years which is a  chapter called scandal on the hill and  it was that and more it was some of the  most interesting for years I've ever had  as a reporter and I had gone off to  cover Bill Clinton and Russert said  you're going to be the chief White House  correspondent covering Bill Clinton but  we can no longer have you on the Today  program doing political analysis once or  twice a week because you can't have the  White House correspondent also doing  analysis because it would you know  confuse people to see you in both roles  that was 1992 which now seems as I write  quaint by standards we all are you know  as as you know I do hardball I do Chris  Matthews Sunday show and happily so I'm  on Meet the Press I'm another broadcast  and we now have a different view of  ourselves but I still always as I know  Brian and others do try to keep as much  distinction as I can between giving  analysis which I think is appropriate  without editorializing or giving  commentary and there is a distinction  and I've had all of these adventures and  it does on cable give us a great  opportunity to share  more insight yes I do don imus and  people ask me what is done I'm as like  and if you listen to him in the morning  you know what Don Imus is probably like  in fact is he's a really smart  interviewer and I've called in from  Afghanistan and Pakistan and Sudan and  other place